11:   the year's product, a pair poster built from books and from asking a person.
12: - **Days.** 29: 24 in units, 2 course-level check days (1 after unit 2, 1 after unit 4) and 3 respond days (1 after
13:   the mid-year check, 2 after the year-end check). One session of about 25 minutes, one session a week, for 29
14:   weeks (APPROACH.md, Pacing and dose: the K to 2 default of 30 sessions of 25 minutes, about 12.5 hours a year, at
15:   the one-a-week end of the settled "one or two sessions a week"). The course sets 29, one fewer than the default,
16:   because of the grade 2 calendar: the year-end check must sit by week 27 (core checks fill weeks 28, 29, 31 and 32),
17:   so the units end in week 26 and only respond days may follow; a third respond day would only fill a week (the
18:   kindergarten read's finding 5), and week 30 is left to the school (For the lead, item 3). The 25 minutes hold
19:   getting to the carpet or the book corner, the session, putting books back and collecting sheets; a lesson plan
20:   budgets about 3 minutes at each end for that, so about 19 working minutes, and every assessment form below is
21:   costed against those 19 minutes for a class of 30. One adult runs the room (the classroom teacher or a librarian);
22:   while the adult checks a small group, the rest of the class works on a book-corner task it has already practiced.
23:   Nine grade 2 rows fit 24 unit days, 6 a unit: units 1, 2 and 4 carry two rows each; unit 3 carries three "with
24:   help" rows that share one case set and one find step (sorting found facts, the two facts that differ, and who made
25:   each source), each a short sorting or circling move. Each unit's days include its end-of-unit assessment and its
26:   respond day (no unit is over 25 days, so no mid-unit assessment). These are course days: with 29 days over 29
27:   weeks, course day n falls in week n of docs/courses/CALENDAR.md, and the scope and sequence places them on school
28:   days.
