196: |---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
197: | 1 | Questions and where to look. *Which of our questions could a source answer, and which kind of source should we try?* | 6 | g2-question-to-research; g2-kinds-of-sources | AASL.IV.A.2 | Connect, wonder, investigate (kinds of sources), express, reflect | Each child writes an "I wonder" of their own on a board card; with the teacher, the class picks a wonder a book could answer and, after saying what they already know (review of AASL.I.A.2, owned in grade 1 by g1-what-i-know), pairs write one question-word question each (who, what, where, when, why, how); the teacher sorts them and children see which ones a source could answer. Matching a question to the kind of source likely to answer it: an information book, a children's encyclopedia, a picture dictionary, a map or a globe, a children's magazine, a person who knows (the school nurse, a gardener), or a page the teacher chose, and saying why (AASL.IV.A.2, owned here: "List the kinds of sources likely to hold the answer you need," checked alone: given three new questions, a fitting kind for each and why). Begun here with help, owned later: turning a topic into questions to research (AASL.I.A.1, owned by g3-research-questions, grade 3). Out: research questions written alone and sorted thin and thick (grade 3); choosing among several sources of one kind (AASL.IV.A.3, grade 5). Days: show-me (hook: a frog in a winter pond picture; the board cards; the teacher turns one wonder into a question-word question, thinking aloud "could a book answer this?"), practice (opening retrieval: a know and wonder sheet on the topic (g1-what-i-know, grade 1); pairs write question-word questions on two new pictures; the teacher's sort), show-me (opening retrieval: story book or information book for a question, and why (g1-story-or-fact-book, grade 1); the source-kind picture cards, matched to questions with a "because"), inquiry (the cycle on the host or stand-alone question: a question from the board, the pair names the kind of source, the teacher hands out that source, the pair finds one fact and draws and writes it with the grade 1 source line; share; self-check), end-of-unit assessment, respond (it opens with the pairs' shares and the self-check, about five minutes, then the regrouped reteach). Skill card: "what do I know, what do I wonder, ask who, what, where..., which kind of source?" pictured; withdrawn on the inquiry day. End-of-unit evidence (about 15 minutes for 30): a sheet item: three new questions read aloud; for each the child circles a source-kind picture and writes a short "because" (g2-kinds-of-sources, about 8 minutes, marked from the page: a fitting kind and a reason that fits it); then a sheet item: a new topic picture (an owl in a tree), and each child writes one question-word question a book could answer (g2-question-to-research, about 4 minutes), then a scanned pointing item: the teacher reads two questions, one a book could answer and one nobody can look up, and children point at the one a book could answer (about 2 minutes). Tools: board cards, source-kind cards, the frog read-aloud, a picture dictionary page, a simple map and a short magazine-style page (Curio Class writes them all). Host: questions about maps and places, social studies grade 2 unit 1 (days 1 to 16), or where rain goes on the school grounds, science grade 2 unit 1 (days 1 to 10); stand-alone: "Where do frogs go in winter?" | First because every later step needs a question a source can answer and a kind of source to try: unit 2 then finds that source on the shelf. The board wonder written by each child opens the year as the kindergarten and grade 1 courses did, and grade 2's first owned id is the kind-of-source choice. Placed in the first weeks beside ELA's question-word work and social studies' maps, whose "where" questions call for a map | Candidate: the continuum's grade 2 cells on recognizing gaps in what one knows (the know half of the retrieval sheet, "what I am not sure of") and on knowing that questions can be answered by finding information (the teacher's sort, said aloud) | Neutral topics only (frogs, owls, places on a map). A "person who knows" is a school staff member by job, never a family member, and no child is asked who at home knows things. A child who writes slowly may say the "because" to the teacher, who writes it (a scribe, APPROACH.md section field 9). Whose voices: the unit records the makers of its read-aloud, pages and pictures (C12) |
198: | 2 | Finding the book, giving the credit. *How do we find the book we need, and how do we tell others where our facts came from?* | 6 | g2-catalog-lookup; g2-credit-line | AASL.VI.B.2 | Investigate (organization, catalog and shelf), express (crediting), reflect | Looking up a book by title or author in a printed catalog card (or an invented catalog screen shown on one shared device), copying its call label, and finding it on the shelf by letter and number order; putting a mixed-up shelf in order; putting a found book back in shelf order and bringing borrowed books back on the set day; the stop rule and the school's own device rules on any device day. Ending every product with a credit line for each source, title and author copied correctly from the cover or the page (AASL.VI.B.2, owned here: "Name who made each work you use and respect their rights," checked alone: a correct credit line for a new book); the credit line is the citation model's simplest form. Begun here with help, owned later: searching more than one kind of source (AASL.IV.B.1, owned by g4-search-more-kinds, grade 4); using information, devices and media responsibly (AASL.VI.A.1, owned by g3-safe-searching, grade 3). Out: key-word lookup (grade 3, g3-key-words); the year and kind of source in a source list (grade 3, g3-source-list). Days: show-me (hook: a fossil book the class needs and cannot see; a catalog card, the call label, the shelf by letter and number), practice (opening retrieval: find the answering page by contents and headings (g1-find-in-a-book, grade 1) and the three stop pictures for one pretend case (g1-device-stop-rule, grade 1); lookups on catalog cards; the mixed-up shelf put in order), show-me (opening retrieval: say the source line for a cover (g1-author-and-title, grade 1); the credit line written from a cover and from a page), inquiry (the cycle on the host or stand-alone question: look up the book on a catalog card, find it on the shelf, find the answering page, draw and write the fact with its credit line, put the book back in order; partner share; self-check), end-of-unit assessment, respond (it opens with the partner shares, about five minutes, then the regrouped reteach). Skill card: "look it up, copy the label, find the letter, find the number; title by author" pictured. End-of-unit evidence (about 13 minutes for 30): a sheet item: a printed catalog entry and a picture of a shelf of spines with call labels; the child circles the named book (g2-catalog-lookup, about 3 minutes); a sheet item: four spine cards numbered in shelf order (about 3 minutes); a sheet item: a credit line written for a new book cover (g2-credit-line, about 4 minutes, marked from the page: title and author, spelled so a reader can find the book); then a scanned pointing item: for a pretend case on a screen, point at the stop steps in order (about 2 minutes). Tools: catalog cards, call labels, spine cards, cover cards, the fossil read-aloud (Curio Class writes them all); an invented catalog screen for a school with one shared device. Host: a fossil book for ela grade 2 unit 2 (days 33 to 74); stand-alone: "What can a fossil tell us?" | After unit 1 because the class now knows the kind of source it wants and needs to find that book; the credit line joins it because the catalog card and the cover hold the same two things, title and author, and every product from here on ends with one. Before unit 3, which finds facts in two books and credits both | Candidate: the continuum's grade 2 cells on the library area that holds a topic (the call label's subject letters said aloud) and on knowing it is wrong to copy from an author or a peer, said with the credit line | Honest use without shaming (APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground, item 4): a product missing its credit line is fixed in the respond day's group, never named in front of the class. Borrowing follows the kindergarten rule (class book corner, no question about books at home, a lost book never a child's blame). Any device day follows the stop rule; the invented catalog screen names no product. Whose voices: the unit records the makers of its books and pictures (C12) |
199: | 3 | Checking facts. *When two books tell us different facts, how do we sort what we found and decide what to trust?* | 6 | g2-fact-sort; g2-two-books-differ; g2-who-made-it | None | Investigate (evidence within sources, makers), construct (sorting), express, reflect | Sorting found facts, drawn or written as a few words on cards, never a copied sentence, into picture boxes that match the question's parts (where it lives, how big it grows, how long it lives), saying why a card belongs; the teacher sets the boxes. Finding two books on the same topic that give a different fact (how long a turtle lives), saying what is the same and what differs, and deciding to check a third source. For a book or an invented web page, naming who made it (a writer, a museum, a store) and why (to teach, to tell a story, to sell). The cycle card gains its fifth picture, "check". Review: the credit line on every card set (unit 2); finding the answering page (grade 1, and unit 2). Begun here with help, owned later: sorting notes by a plan one can explain (AASL.IV.B.4, owned by g4-evidence-notes, grade 4); comparing how several sources treat the same question (AASL.II.B.2, owned by g7-sources-disagree, grade 7); questioning each source's maker and purpose before trusting it (AASL.IV.B.3, owned by g5-first-lateral-check, grade 5). Out: notes in boxes the student plans (grade 3, g3-notes-in-boxes); checking a real site by leaving it (grade 5, lateral reading); sources that disagree on views (grade 7; K to 5 keeps to facts). Days: show-me (hook: two turtle books that disagree on how long a turtle lives; the picture boxes and the sort, thinking aloud), show-me (opening retrieval: find the answering page by its heading in two books (g1-find-in-a-book, grade 1); the two facts, same and different, and a third source; who made each book or page and why; the "check" picture), practice (opening retrieval: a credit line for a cover (g2-credit-line, unit 2); sort new fact cards; compare two short passages; maker and purpose for three invented pages), inquiry (the cycle on the host or stand-alone question: facts from two sources into the boxes, the fact that differs, a third source checked, the maker of each source named; credit lines; share; self-check), end-of-unit assessment, respond (it opens with the class box mat shared and the self-check, about five minutes, then the regrouped reteach). Skill card: "sort it, spot the difference, check a third, who made it and why?" pictured. End-of-unit evidence (about 15 minutes for 30): a sheet item: four new fact cards cut and glued into two or three boxes on a mat (g2-fact-sort, about 5 minutes, marked from the page: each card in a fitting box); a sheet item: two short passages read aloud that disagree on one fact; the child circles the two facts and ticks "check a third source" (g2-two-books-differ, about 5 minutes); a sheet item: an invented book cover and an invented page, and for each the child circles who made it and why, teach, story or sell (g2-who-made-it, about 4 minutes). Tools: fact cards, box mats, paired passages, invented pages on paper, the turtle read-aloud, the five-picture cycle card (Curio Class writes them all). Host: what plants need, s2-plant-needs-test in science grade 2 unit 5 (days 48 to 56), taught in the same weeks; or where water is found, s2-water-where in science grade 2 unit 1 (days 1 to 10), as review; stand-alone: "How long does a turtle live?" | After unit 2 because the class can now find and credit two books, so it can compare them; the three moves share one find step on one case set, so they are taught together as the cycle's new "check" picture. Placed in weeks 15 to 20, beside science's plant needs unit and social studies' two accounts (unit 5), and after social studies' source makers and ELA's author's purpose, so the maker question meets words the class has heard | Candidate: the continuum's grade 2 cells on recognizing that not all information on the internet is true (said on the invented store page) and on taking notes in words, phrases and drawings, not whole sentences (the fact card rule) | Neutral differences only (APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground, item 1; the g2-two-books-differ teacher note): the two books disagree on facts about animals, plants, weather and places, never on views on politics, religion, identity groups or conflict. The invented store page sells a plain thing (seed packets) and names no company. Whose voices: the unit records the makers of its passages and pages (C12) |
200: | 4 | Our research poster. *What can we find out by reading and by asking a person, and how do we share it?* | 6 | g2-research-share; g2-group-research | None | Connect, investigate (asking people), construct, express (product and audience), reflect | A research group plans two questions about the class topic to ask another person (a classmate or a school staff member, or a family member at home only if the family chooses), the teacher checks the questions first, the group asks them and brings back the answers, then says what each member added. Pairs run the cycle on the class question and make a poster with two found facts (from a book or from the person), a picture and a credit line for each source, share it with another pair, and say back what the other poster taught them; each child then points to the pictured step that went best and the one to do better. Review: the fact sort (unit 3) for the facts the group brings back; the credit line (unit 2). Begun here with help, owned later: making a product that shows what the research taught (AASL.I.B.3, owned by g3-make-product, grade 3); responding to a classmate's work (AASL.I.C.1, owned by g3-gallery-feedback, grade 3); looking back on how a search went (AASL.I.D.4, owned by g5-search-log-look-back, grade 5); speaking up with findings and naming what each classmate adds (AASL.III.D.1 and AASL.II.A.1, owned by g3-research-team, grade 3); asking others how they see a topic (AASL.II.D.2, owned by g4-asking-others, grade 4). Out: a product made alone from one's own notes (grade 3); written feedback notes (grade 3); a class survey (grade 4). Days: show-me (hook: a pretend class's poster; the poster template; planning two questions for a person with the question frame), inquiry (opening retrieval: pictured jobs and what each helper added (g1-group-helpers, grade 1); groups finish their questions, the teacher checks them, and a visiting staff member answers each group in turn), inquiry (opening retrieval: one class book page and one star and wish on a pretend page (g1-class-book, g1-star-and-wish, grade 1); pairs find a fact in a book, sort the facts, and draft the poster with credit lines), inquiry (each pair shares with another pair and says back what the other poster taught them; a short fix to the poster; the spoken look-back), end-of-unit assessment, respond (it opens with the posters on the wall and the self-check, about five minutes, then the regrouped reteach). Skill card: "plan two questions, ask, listen, bring it back; two facts, a picture, a credit line" pictured. End-of-unit evidence (about 17 minutes for 30): a sheet item: a pretend class's poster not seen before; each child writes one thing it taught them, from the poster (g2-research-share, about 4 minutes, marked from the page); then a station of three at the check table with a new topic card while the others finish poster fixes in the book corner: the group plans two questions to ask a person (questions on the topic, asked of a person, none about home or family) and each child names what one member added (g2-group-research, about 1.3 minutes a group, ten groups, about 13 minutes). Tools: the question frame, the poster template and poster paper, the pretend posters, the pollinator read-aloud (Curio Class writes them). Host: the pollination research of ela grade 2 unit 3 (days 78 to 118) (e2-research), or seeds and pollinators, science grade 2 unit 6 (days 60 to 65); stand-alone: "How do people at our school care for its plants?" | Last because it gathers the year: a question a source can answer (unit 1), the book found and credited (unit 2) and facts sorted and checked (unit 3) are what each pair brings to its poster, and asking a person adds the year's new kind of source (unit 1's "person who knows"). Placed in weeks 21 to 26 beside ELA's pollination research and social studies' interview unit (unit 6), so the class has met asking people and its question is real | Candidate: the continuum's grade 2 cells on a first sense of audience (who the poster is for, said before drafting) and on asking others to explain their ideas and offering one's own (the group's follow-up question) | Interviews (APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground, item 8): the topic stays on the school's plants or pollinators; questions are checked by the teacher before use; no one is asked about family history, home, beliefs, identity, health, money, food or meals, a family member's job, or status; any staff member stands in for a family member, and the family interview is optional and never a condition of the grade; answers are reported without a full name ("our school's gardener"). Groups are mixed by the teacher, never by background. Whose voices: the unit records whose work and words its sources and interviews show (C12) |
