197: | 1 | Questions and where to look. *Which of our questions could a source answer, and which kind of source should we try?* | 6 | g2-question-to-research; g2-kinds-of-sources | AASL.IV.A.2 | Connect, wonder, investigate (kinds of sources), express, reflect | Each child writes an "I wonder" of their own on a board card; with the teacher, the class picks a wonder a book could answer and, after saying what they already know (review of AASL.I.A.2, owned in grade 1 by g1-what-i-know), pairs write one question-word question each (who, what, where, when, why, how); the teacher sorts them and children see which ones a source could answer. Matching a question to the kind of source likely to answer it: an information book, a children's encyclopedia, a picture dictionary, a map or a globe, a children's magazine, a person who knows (the school nurse, a gardener), or a page the teacher chose, and saying why (AASL.IV.A.2, owned here: "List the kinds of sources likely to hold the answer you need," checked alone: given three new questions, a fitting kind for each and why). Begun here with help, owned later: turning a topic into questions to research (AASL.I.A.1, owned by g3-research-questions, grade 3). Out: research questions written alone and sorted thin and thick (grade 3); choosing among several sources of one kind (AASL.IV.A.3, grade 5). Days: show-me (hook: a frog in a winter pond picture; the board cards; the teacher turns one wonder into a question-word question, thinking aloud "could a book answer this?"), practice (opening retrieval: a know and wonder sheet on the topic (g1-what-i-know, grade 1); pairs write question-word questions on two new pictures; the teacher's sort), show-me (opening retrieval: story book or information book for a question, and why (g1-story-or-fact-book, grade 1); the source-kind picture cards, matched to questions with a "because"), inquiry (the cycle on the host or stand-alone question: a question from the board, the pair names the kind of source, the teacher hands out that source, the pair finds one fact and draws and writes it with the grade 1 source line; share; self-check), end-of-unit assessment, respond (it opens with the pairs' shares and the self-check, about five minutes, then the regrouped reteach). Skill card: "what do I know, what do I wonder, ask who, what, where..., which kind of source?" pictured; withdrawn on the inquiry day. End-of-unit evidence (about 15 minutes for 30): a sheet item: three new questions read aloud; for each the child circles a source-kind picture and writes a short "because" (g2-kinds-of-sources, about 8 minutes, marked from the page: a fitting kind and a reason that fits it); then a sheet item: a new topic picture (an owl in a tree), and each child writes one question-word question a book could answer (g2-question-to-research, about 4 minutes), then a scanned pointing item: the teacher reads two questions, one a book could answer and one nobody can look up, and children point at the one a book could answer (about 2 minutes). Tools: board cards, source-kind cards, the frog read-aloud, a picture dictionary page, a simple map and a short magazine-style page (Curio Class writes them all). Host: questions about maps and places, social studies grade 2 unit 1 (days 1 to 16), or where rain goes on the school grounds, science grade 2 unit 1 (days 1 to 10); stand-alone: "Where do frogs go in winter?" | First because every later step needs a question a source can answer and a kind of source to try: unit 2 then finds that source on the shelf. The board wonder written by each child opens the year as the kindergarten and grade 1 courses did, and grade 2's first owned id is the kind-of-source choice. Placed in the first weeks beside ELA's question-word work and social studies' maps, whose "where" questions call for a map | Candidate: the continuum's grade 2 cells on recognizing gaps in what one knows (the know half of the retrieval sheet, "what I am not sure of") and on knowing that questions can be answered by finding information (the teacher's sort, said aloud) | Neutral topics only (frogs, owls, places on a map). A "person who knows" is a school staff member by job, never a family member, and no child is asked who at home knows things. A child who writes slowly may say the "because" to the teacher, who writes it (a scribe, APPROACH.md section field 9). Whose voices: the unit records the makers of its read-aloud, pages and pictures (C12) |
