3: Course `library-and-research-skills-grade-3` (catalog id `library-3`), grade 3. Draft frame, 2026-11-25, written to the library pilot frame (kindergarten); the scope and sequence sets the days later.
4: 
5: ## The course in brief
6: 
7: - **Units.** 4, one year of library and research skills, each unit one full inquiry cycle (ask, find, judge, use,
8:   share, reflect) on a small question, in the order a grade 3 class needs the moves: turning a topic into research
9:   questions first, then searching for them safely by key word, then telling kinds of sources apart and making notes
10:   in boxes, then researching as a team and making, crediting and giving feedback on a product.
11: - **Days.** 30: 26 in units, 2 course-level check days (1 after unit 2, 1 after unit 4) and 2 respond days (1 after
12:   each check). One session of about 35 minutes, one session a week, for 30 weeks (APPROACH.md, Pacing and dose: the
13:   3 to 5 default of 30 sessions of 35 minutes, about 17.5 hours a year, at the one-a-week end of the settled "one or
14:   two sessions a week"). The 35 minutes hold getting out notebooks and source sets, the session, putting books and
15:   cards back and collecting work; a lesson plan budgets about 3 minutes at each end for that, so about 29 working
16:   minutes, and every assessment form below is costed against those 29 minutes for a class of 30. One adult runs the
17:   room (the classroom teacher or a librarian). Nine grade 3 rows fit 26 unit days by load: unit 1 carries one owned
18:   row and gets 5 days; unit 2 two rows (one owned, one bringing in the search plan model) and gets 7; unit 3 two
19:   carried rows done with help and gets 6; unit 4 four rows (three owned, four ids) and gets 8, since it also holds the
20:   year's product, its draft, the gallery feedback and the revision. Each unit's days include its end-of-unit
21:   assessment (no unit is over 25 days, so no mid-unit assessment); units 1 to 3 also hold their respond day, and unit
22:   4's respond step is its revision day, with its regrouped reteach in the year-end check's respond day. These are course days: with
23:   30 days over 30 weeks, course day n falls in week n of docs/courses/CALENDAR.md, and the scope and sequence places
24:   them on school days.
25: - **Day kinds.** In library's words (APPROACH.md, The kinds of day; docs/GLOSSARY.md, teaching day):
26:   Understanding is the show-me day (the teacher thinks aloud through the move on an invented case, holding the unit's
27:   skill card); Fluency is the practice day (short, varied tries of the same move on new cases, with feedback: sort six
28:   questions, pull key words from three questions, sort a source set, fix two source lists); Application is the
29:   inquiry day (the class runs part of the cycle on its host question: question, search, judge, note, make, share).
30:   Fluency is modest in grade 3: one or two practice days in units 1 to 3, a practice half-day in unit 4, plus the five-minute
31:   opening retrieval on later days. Every unit's day 1 is a show-me day with the hook and the compelling question.
32: - **Standard.** AASL National School Library Standards, second edition, learner framework (ids and our own words
33:   only, data/standards/aasl-words.txt; never the competency text, and no claim of alignment approved by anyone).
34:   Ownership rule (STANDARD.md section 3): an id is owned once, in the school year where it is first checked alone.
35:   Grade 3 owns seven ids (AASL.I.A.1; AASL.VI.A.1; AASL.I.B.3; AASL.I.C.1 and AASL.I.C.2; AASL.III.D.1 and
36:   AASL.II.A.1) and carries seven as `~`, each named in its unit's What students learn with its owner. Skills: the
37:   nine progression rows, each in one unit. Check set: the Empire State Information Fluency Continuum, 3 to 5 band
38:   file, grade 3 column and its bold priority skills (read cell by cell for this frame, later.csv L103; result in the
39:   Check set read bullet); the "Check set adds" cells name what each unit plan should weigh from it. Values: K to 5
40:   case sets stay neutral (no politics, elections, religion, identity groups or conflict; Cory, 2026-11-21).
41: - **Base text.** None. Every day is Curio Class's own. Curio Class writes: a short background passage and a
42:   six-source set for each unit's stand-alone question (passages, a photo with caption, a log, a diagram), an invented
43:   printed catalog (title, author and key word pages) and an invented results page, a printed map of an invented
44:   library's main areas, the question sort cards, the scenario cards for safe searching, the note box sheet, the team
45:   sheet, the source list model card, the gallery note slips, one skill card a unit and the research notebook pages. No
46:   day needs a trade book, a paid source, a library visit or a database (no library access is assumed; Cory,
47:   2026-11-11).
48: - **Host tasks.** Every unit runs its cycle on a host task from another grade 3 course, named by its row id for the
49:   teacher to schedule against, and carries a stand-alone host question of its own (an invented question with a closed
50:   source set of Curio Class passages and pictures) so the unit runs when no core task is in step or at home
51:   (APPROACH.md). The scope and sequence records both. A host is used for its question only when its topic keeps the
52:   K to 5 neutral rule; otherwise the unit borrows only the skill and runs on its stand-alone case (unit 3, below).
53: - **The inquiry arc.** Each unit opens with its compelling question and closes with the share step and the
54:   self-check: each student rates their own use of the skill card's steps on a three-face scale, names one step they
55:   did well and one thing to do differently next time (APPROACH.md, the form of daily evidence; this answers the
56:   continuum's grade 3 reflect skill, own strengths and a goal). The lens's six steps (ask, find, judge, use, share,
57:   reflect) are now named on the cycle card in grade 3: ask is the research question (unit 1), find the key-word
58:   search (unit 2), judge the source's kind, maker and a second-source check (unit 3), use the notes in boxes (unit 3)
59:   and the product (unit 4), share the gallery walk (unit 4).
60: - **Evidence.** Daily: an exit ticket that asks the student to do the unit's move once on a new case (about 5
61:   minutes, all at once), with error letters for the usual slips; on inquiry days, the working notes in the research
62:   notebook (APPROACH.md). End-of-unit assessment: a written sitting on a case not seen before, all 30 at once, scored
63:   on a short teacher-judged rubric plus a few one-answer items checked on the page, in about 20 to 25 minutes; the
64:   team skill in unit 4 is observed at team tables instead (about 4 minutes a team of four or five, about 28 minutes
65:   for seven teams, on its own day). Each unit names its form and total below; no unit assessment or course-level
66:   check spills into its respond day. It is scored on a day after the move was practiced, never on a show-me day. The
67:   respond day's groups come from the class record sheet by the rubric step missed (APPROACH.md, unit field 7).
68: - **Reading level.** Source passages at about grade 3 level, 150 to 300 words, with a read-aloud copy on the teacher
69:   page and a shorter set for students who read below it (APPROACH.md, section field 9). Students write short notes,
70:   questions and lists; the product's writing load stays within ELA grade 3's informative writing.
71: - **Paper path.** The whole course runs on paper: printed source sets, the invented catalog and results pages,
72:   scenario cards, notebooks and the source list model. No day needs a device, an internet connection or a student
73:   account.
74: - **Devices.** None per student, and no owned or carried code needs technology. Where a school has one shared device
75:   or a projector, unit 2 may show the invented catalog on it and run the turn routine; the printed catalog and a
76:   pretend device card do the same job in full, so a paper-path student meets AASL.VI.A.1 (the stop rule, turns,
77:   never typing a name, school, place or photo, care of books and devices) completely on scenario cards.
78: - **Safety read.** No hands-on materials beyond books, paper, cards and pencils, no field work and no trip, so no
79:   laboratory or field guide applies. The screen-safety side is the stop rule and the private-information rule,
80:   checked by the sensitivity read (APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground, items 2 and 3).
81: - **Sensitivity reads.** Every unit gets the fresh sensitivity agent's read (docs/pipeline/briefs/sensitivity-read.md,
82:   kind `sensitivity`) on its case set at the unit plan and again at the days; no outside human reader is planned
83:   (Cory, 2026-11-14). No unit carries cultural, Native, hard-history, health or disability content: the case sets are
84:   spiders, the moon, storms and weather records, and pond life. What the agent studies first: APPROACH.md "Sensitive
85:   ground" items 1 (neutral K to 5 case sets; unit 3 does not borrow social studies unit 4's Native nations content),
86:   2 (the stop rule, unit 2), 3 (privacy, unit 2), 4 (honest use without shaming, unit 4's source list) and 5 (whose
87:   voices a source set shows, C12); and, for unit 2, the National Center for Missing and Exploited Children's free
88:   grades 3 to 5 presentation "Being a Good Digital Citizen: Grades 3-5", listed at
89:   https://www.missingkids.org/netsmartz/resources (listing read 2026-11-25; the kit was not opened, and a download may
90:   need a sign-in). No organization's name goes on a student, teacher or family page.
91: - **Supplies and cost.** Printing at about 5 cents a page (ours, provisional) for a class of 30: unit 1 about $6
92:   (background passage, question sort cards, three exit tickets), unit 2 about $9 (the printed catalog in pairs,
93:   results page, library map, scenario cards reused all year), unit 3 about $8 (the source set in pairs, note box
94:   sheets), unit 4 about $10 (team source sets, team sheets, gallery slips, product paper); about $33 a year. A
95:   research notebook is any folded stack of paper.
96: - **Check set read (L103, 2026-11-25).** The 3 to 5 band file's grade 3 column and priority boxes, every indicator
97:   row (S:\CurioClass-sources\library\aasl-2026-11-21\esifc-3-5\esifc-priority-3-5.docx, read as text with
98:   `python -I`, nothing copied). Met by the rows: questions formed with guidance (g3-research-questions); one or two
99:   key words and a catalog search by author, title and key word with help (g3-key-words); simple note-making in a
100:   graphic organizer and the main idea with details (g3-notes-in-boxes); a simple author and title source list
101:   (g3-source-list, which adds year and kind, the grade 4 cell, one year early); choosing a product format and
102:   presenting main points clearly (g3-make-product); respect for and response to others' ideas (g3-gallery-feedback);
103:   contributing to group talk (g3-research-team); privacy and keeping to safe sites (g3-safe-searching). Differences,
104:   each a row change listed in For the lead item 1: a background read from a teacher-given source before the questions
