FILE docs/courses/english-language-arts-grade-2/frame.md
## Units

| # | Unit and compelling question | Days | Skills | Owned standards | C3 | What students learn | Why this unit, here | Check set adds | Sensitive topics and teacher notes |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Schools and community. *How are schools around the world alike and different?* | 32 | e2-vowel-teams; e2-fluency; e2-wh-questions; e2-character-response; e2-images-and-text; e2-informative; e2-discussion; e2-spelling | RF.2.3a; RF.2.3b; RF.2.4; RL.2.10; RI.2.10; RL.2.1; RI.2.1; SL.2.2; RL.2.3; RL.2.7; RI.2.7; W.2.2; SL.2.1; SL.2.3; L.2.2d; L.2.2e; L.2.2 | None (ELA) | Topic block: the year's long text, a chapter-length Curio Class story of a girl who changes schools mid-year and finds her place, read whole across two weeks; informational texts on schools in several countries (Curio Class texts with cleared photographs and maps); a poem about the school day. Who, what, where, when, why and how questions answered from the text (SL.2.2 with them); how characters respond to events and challenges (story-map, review); what illustrations, photographs and diagrams add (RL.2.7, RI.2.7); gaining the floor, linking to others' comments and asking for clarification in named protocols (SL.2.1a to c). Writing: the performance task, an informational book on the most important thing about schools, with a topic, facts and definitions and a concluding statement (paragraph-frame and main-idea-details, review), through the process. Foundations block: retrieval and a short placement screen in week 1; the syllable types and the long-vowel teams from grade 1 reviewed (Skills Block cycles 1 to 5); e2-vowel-teams continues in units 2 and 3, where the additional teams are taught (RF.2.3b); spelling patterns generalized and first use of a beginning dictionary; fluency in grade 2 text, read with purpose, aloud with accuracy, rate and expression on rereading, and with context used to confirm or self-correct (RF.2.4a to c), continued all year (e2-fluency, e2-spelling). Lettered parts of these standards taught in another unit: L.2.2a, L.2.2b, L.2.2c (unit 2). | The base text's module 1, six weeks, built for launching routines. The long text comes first in grade 2 because it extends grade 1's whole chapter story through a new school story, sustained rereading and more independent text-based responses, and the class needs the whole year to grow on it; a school story suits the first weeks. Vowel teams review grade 1 before the new teams of units 2 and 3. Same-year social studies leans on the school texts (cross-subject). | Candidate: the base text's module 1 unit assessments, adapted (questions on a text about schools; how a character responds; the informational book) and the Skills Block cycle 1 to 5 checks, on reviewed patterns only; checked in the unit plan. | Schools abroad are shown as everyday places, not as poor or exotic, named by country, and read by the sensitivity agent after its study (brief). A new-school story may touch a child who has moved or lives in shelter: home is not probed. No child's school record or photograph leaves the room. |
| 2 | Fossils tell of Earth's changes. *What can fossils tell us about the past?* | 42 | e2-tricky-spellings; e2-prefixes-suffixes; e2-story-structure; e2-narrative; e2-revise-edit; e2-word-strategies; e2-author-purpose; e2-text-features; e2-grammar; e2-main-topic-paragraphs; e2-sentences | RF.2.3e; RF.2.3f; RF.2.3; RF.2.3d; RL.2.5; W.2.3; W.2.5; W.2.6; RI.2.4; L.2.4a; L.2.4b; L.2.4c; L.2.4d; L.2.4e; L.2.4; RI.2.6; RI.2.5; L.2.1a; L.2.1b; L.2.1c; L.2.1d; L.2.1e; L.2.1; RI.2.2; L.2.1f; L.2.2a; L.2.2b; L.2.2c | None (ELA) | Topic block: informational texts on how fossils form and what they show (Curio Class texts with labeled diagrams), a true account in Curio Class words of Mary Anning, who found fossils on the cliffs of the English coast as a girl, and a Curio Class story of a child on a fossil dig. Time is said as "very long ago", with no number scale. The main purpose of a text; the main topic of a multiparagraph text and the focus of each paragraph (main-idea-details, review); captions, bold print, subheadings, glossaries and indexes used to find facts; how a story's beginning introduces and its ending concludes (story-map and narrative-arc, review); context, prefixes, roots, compound words, and glossaries and beginning dictionaries, both print and digital, for unknown words (L.2.4e; context-clues and word-parts, review). Writing: the performance task, an illustrated narrative of one well-elaborated event (discovering a fossil), with actions, thoughts and feelings, time words and a sense of closure, revised and then edited as two steps and published on paper and as one class digital piece typed on a shared device (writing-process, review; W.2.5, W.2.6), with drawings that clarify the story (SL.2.5 begun). Language: collective nouns, irregular plurals and past tenses, reflexive pronouns, adjectives and adverbs; expanding and rearranging sentences; capitals for holidays, product names (invented ones only) and geographic names (L.2.2a), commas in the greetings and closings of letters (L.2.2b), apostrophes. Foundations block: r-controlled vowels reviewed (cycle 6), then new teaching: oi, oy, ou and ow with not-contractions (cycle 7), ild, old, ind and ost (cycle 8), doubling before a suffix (cycle 9), the three sounds of -ed (cycle 10), oo, ou, ui, ue and ew (cycle 11), and -tion and -sion (cycle 12); grade 2 irregular words. e2-vowel-teams continued (cycles 7 and 11); e2-tricky-spellings and e2-prefixes-suffixes open here with the suffixes and continue in units 3 and 4, where the prefixes are taught. Lettered parts of these standards taught in another unit: RF.2.3a (unit 1); RF.2.3b (unit 1, unit 3); RF.2.3c (unit 3). | The base text's module 2. Fossil texts are built with paragraphs, headings and glossaries, so the informational structure rows sit here; a fossil find gives the narrative a real event. Suffixes come with the word strategies they serve; the decoded prefixes follow in units 3 and 4, in the Skills Block's order. Grammar and editing are checked here so the second half of the year uses them. | Candidate: the base text's module 2 unit assessments, adapted (main topic and purpose of a fossil text; word strategies; the fossil narrative, revised and edited) and the Skills Block checks for cycles 6 to 12 (no prefixes yet); checked in the unit plan. | Earth's age is said as "very long ago"; no child is asked to weigh it against family or faith beliefs; the science is stated plainly. Mary Anning's hard life is told at grade level (her family was poor; she was not let into the Geological Society of London), without dwelling on loss. Product names in the capital-letters practice are invented, never real brands. |
| 3 | The secret world of pollination. *How do plants and pollinators help each other?* | 41 | e2-two-syllable-words; e2-connections-series; e2-research; e2-tell-and-record; e2-word-relationships | RF.2.3c; RI.2.3; W.2.7; W.2.8; SL.2.4; SL.2.5; SL.2.6; L.2.3; L.2.5; L.2.6 | None (ELA) | Topic block: informational texts on how pollination works, step by step, and on bees, butterflies, bats and hummingbirds (Curio Class texts, drawing on recent US government pages for children on pollinators), a Curio Class story told from a bee's side as a second anchor, and poems. A series of events or steps in a text (text-structure, review: first the bee lands, then pollen sticks, next ...); real-life connections and shades of meaning among closely related verbs and adjectives (L.2.5a, b; L.2.6); harder band texts read by students with support. Writing and speaking: shared research on one pollinator from provided sources alone, with notes (research-cycle, review; W.2.7, W.2.8), written up as an informative paragraph, planned, drafted, revised and edited (W.2.2 and W.2.5, review), then the performance task: a poster with labeled drawings and a short oral presentation of the research. Then a story told from a pollinator's view (the bee story's model), told with facts and relevant details in coherent sentences (SL.2.4), recorded as audio with the teacher on a shared device and given the child's drawings to clarify it (SL.2.5), in complete sentences (SL.2.6); two versions of one line, the formal recorded one and the informal pair-talk one, compared to say what changes (L.2.3a). Foundations block: consonant-le in two-syllable words (cycles 13 and 14, syllable-types, review), -k, -ck and -ic and the prefixes un- and re- (cycle 15), -dge and -ge (cycle 16), -ch and -tch with -er and -est (cycle 17), -ible and -able (cycle 18), review with au and aw (cycle 19). e2-vowel-teams continued (cycle 19), completing RF.2.3b; e2-tricky-spellings and e2-prefixes-suffixes continued. | The base text's module 3, its research module. Pollination is a sequence, so the series row sits here; gathering from provided sources prepares grade 3's short research. The written paragraph and presentation are the base text's own sequence; the recorded story comes after a half year of class talk and gives SL.2.4 and SL.2.5 a story, as the standards name, and the two take about 2 of the unit's 3 spare days. Unit 4 builds on this unit's pollinator knowledge. | Candidate: the base text's module 3 unit assessments, adapted (describing the steps of pollination; research notes into a new informational paragraph; the poster and presentation; the recorded pollinator story with drawings) and the Skills Block checks for cycles 13 to 19; checked in the unit plan. | Bee stings and allergies: any garden visit is supervised, with allergies checked with families. Recordings stay in the class; no child's voice or name is posted outside it, and a child may record a part with a partner or present live instead of being recorded alone. |
| 4 | Providing for pollinators. *How can people help pollinators thrive?* | 39 | e2-fables-folktales; e2-points-of-view; e2-compare-texts; e2-reasons-support; e2-opinion; e2-rhythm-and-meaning | RL.2.2; RL.2.6; RL.2.9; RI.2.9; RI.2.8; W.2.1; RL.2.4 | None (ELA) | Topic block: fables and folktales from diverse cultures, each told in Curio Class words and credited: two versions of the ant and grasshopper story (after Joseph Jacobs, 1894, and La Fontaine, translated by Elizur Wright, 1882 edition, book I fable I), a story of a hummingbird that carries water to a forest fire, told in Quechua tradition among others (its origin is given variously in print, so it is credited with care), and a Japanese folktale, the old man who made withered trees to flower, retold in Curio Class words from a public-domain edition (Yei Theodora Ozaki's; Cory, 2026-11-24, after the Mexican lizard and sun tale could not be cleared); informational texts on how people help pollinators (gardens, fewer sprays, habitat). Each tale's lesson or moral (story-map, review); characters' points of view, voiced in readers' theatre; two versions of a story compared, and the most important points of two texts on helping pollinators; beats, rhyme, alliteration and repeated lines in poems and songs (figurative-language, review); how reasons support an author's points (claim-evidence-reasoning, review). Writing: the performance task, a wildflower seed packet giving an opinion on why to plant the flower, with reasons from the unit's texts, linking words and a concluding statement and a drawing (paragraph-frame, review), through the process; students first return to gathering facts from the provided sources (W.2.8, review). Foundations block, new teaching: y to i before -es (cycle 20), schwa spelled a with the prefixes de- and dis- (cycle 21), schwa spelled e and o with im- and in- (cycle 22), -ate (cycle 23), compound words (cycle 24), -cal and -cle (cycle 25), -ous and -us (cycle 26); then cumulative review and end-of-year fluency (e2-two-syllable-words, e2-tricky-spellings and e2-prefixes-suffixes continued and completed). | The base text's module 4. Reasons-support comes before the opinion piece in the unit, and two versions of a tale come after a year of story-map work, so comparing is built on secure retelling. Tales from several cultures widen the year's traditions. The year ends with the most independent writing. | Candidate: the base text's module 4 unit assessments, adapted (the moral of a tale; comparing two versions; the seed packet opinion) and the Skills Block end-of-year check, on all grade 2 patterns including cycles 20 to 26; checked in the unit plan. | Tales from living cultures are named by people and place, told with respect and read by the sensitivity agent after it studies each tradition's own sources (brief). A forest fire in the hummingbird tale is told without danger to people. Homes differ: no child is asked about a yard; planting is a class pot or school bed. |


FILE docs/courses/science-grade-2/frame.md
## Units

| # | Unit and compelling question | Days | Skills | Owned standards | C3 | What students learn | Why this unit, here | Check set adds | Sensitive topics and teacher notes |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Land and water. *After a rainstorm, where does the water on our schoolyard go?* Anchor: puddles and a stream running along the curb after rain, photographed or watched from the door | 10 | s2-land-water-model; s2-water-where | 2-ESS2-2; 2-ESS2-3 | Practices: developing and using a model; obtaining information from texts, maps and video. Crosscutting: patterns | A model (a sand-tray landscape, clay relief or picture map with a key) of the shapes and kinds of land and bodies of water in an area: the local area first, then a contrasting one (a coastal area if the school is inland, an inland one if it is on the coast); the pattern that rivers run downhill to lakes or oceans; no quantitative scaling. Then information from texts, maps and video on where water is found on Earth (oceans, rivers, lakes, ponds, ice and snow) and that it can be solid or liquid; facts from two sources recorded on one chart. Before the chart, a short supported source choice: of two supplied sources, which can answer the scientific question (a page that shows where water is found and how that was learned, against one that gives an opinion or a made-up story), and why; the unit's end task includes one source-choice item answered alone. No water cycle and no amounts, and no internet searching by students | First because the schoolyard water after rain is an easy first-hand anchor in fall, and because social studies grade 2 uses this modeling in its world-features unit from about week 6: science teaches it in weeks 1 to 4. The two rows join: the model shows where the water runs, the sources show where it ends up | Candidate: settled, keep: school-grounds picture map on day 4, one day inside the ten; compare the representation with the real place | Outdoor looks after rain: stay on paths, no wading in runoff or drains. Water spills wiped at once. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 2 | The land changes, fast and slow. *Why has the bare path down our slope become a ditch while the grass beside it has not, and how could we stop it?* Anchor: a bare, worn path on a schoolyard slope (or a photograph of one) beside grass that holds | 16 | s2-fast-slow-events; s2-erosion-solutions; s2-compare-designs | 2-ESS1-1; 2-ESS2-1; K-2-ETS1-3 | Practices: obtaining information from several sources; designing and comparing solutions; analysing data with measurement. Crosscutting: stability and change (introduced); cause and effect | Information from several sources (photographs, video, Curio Class's own short texts) as evidence that some Earth events happen quickly (a volcano erupting, an earthquake, a landslide, a flood) and others slowly (a riverbank worn away, a rock worn smooth): both clarification kinds, no measured time scales. Picks: Kilauea's lava flows and a landslide across a mountain road (USGS) for fast; a worn stone step and a sea cliff worn back by waves over many years for slow (no canyon here: a canyon is grade 4 unit 4's anchor). Then solutions that slow or stop wind or water from changing the land: in a sand tray, walls, rock barriers (the clarification's dikes), grass mats and planted seedlings (shrubs, grass, trees) and stick windbreaks, tested against poured water or a fan on low; the class measures in whole centimeters how far the land wore back. K-2-ETS1-3 in full: two designs tested on the same problem, the data compared, and what each does well and poorly said | Fast and slow events come first because they introduce stability-change, which the erosion designs use. The anchor follows unit 1's runoff: the water that ran across the schoolyard also moved the land. K-2-ETS1-3 sits here because the barrier tests are the year's first two-design comparison; unit 3 practices it again as review. It opens with the path hook, then a brief return to kindergarten severe weather (what was coming, preparation and response) before sorting fast and slow events, a short supported repair guided by the day 11 retrieval; no readiness check sits on day 10. Fall, with matched photographs if frozen | Candidate: settled, keep: real protected-hillside before/after pair on day 24, one existing day; ten-minute transfer after its independent solution comparison | Disaster images can upset children who have lived through one: choose images without injured people, stress helpers and safety, never ask a child to retell an event. The sensitivity agent studies young children and disasters first (brief). Fans on low with fingers clear; water wiped up at once; hands washed after soil. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 3 | Materials and their best uses. *Why are rain boots made of rubber and mittens of wool, and not the other way round?* Design question: which material makes the best rain hat for the class's paper doll? Anchor: the class's paper doll, left by the window in a drizzle in a paper hat, comes back soggy, and the class tests rubber, wool, foil, cloth and plastic to find which hat keeps it dry | 12 | s2-material-properties; s2-best-material | 2-PS1-1; 2-PS1-2 | Practices: planning and carrying out an investigation; analysing data; argument from evidence. Crosscutting: patterns; cause and effect (a test is designed to show which material causes the dry hat); structure and function | Plan and carry out tests (scratch, bend, press, shine a light through) to describe and sort materials (wood, metal, plastic, fabric, paper, rubber; glass shown, not handled) by observable properties: color, texture, hardness, flexibility and whether light passes through (all the clarification's properties, plus grade 1's light). The same material under cooler and warmer conditions: water and ice observed, their properties recorded and each sample classified as solid or liquid, so a material's observable properties can depend on temperature (warmer or cooler in words only; the adult prepares the ice and warm water); the unit's end task includes classifying one material shown at two temperatures. Patterns: different objects share a property; one material is found in many objects. Then test data decide which material best suits a purpose, across all five clarification properties: absorbency (which paper towel soaks up most water; the rain hat), strength and flexibility (which material makes the strongest bridge deck, how far it bent), hardness and texture. Lengths only as measures; students draw the bar graph and argue for a choice, comparing two candidates' strengths and weaknesses (unit 2's skill, as review) | After unit 2 because the choice of material uses the compare step K-2-ETS1-3 just taught; sorting comes before choosing because a property must be named before it can be tested for a purpose. Indoor work for late fall | Candidate: settled, drop: pencil dismantling, zero days; it adds composition and sharp parts rather than required property evidence | No glass or sharp metal in student hands. Water wiped up at once. Fabric and paper samples are new, never brought from home. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). Anchor source: our own, from the frame's design question; OpenSciEd elementary unit 2.2 (designing a toy from classroom materials) was read and not used, since its toy problem leans on 2-PS1-3 rather than this unit's 2-PS1-1 and 2-PS1-2. |
| 4 | Can we change it back? *Our block castle became a boat, and melted chocolate hardened again, but a cooked egg never runs again: which changes can be undone?* Anchor: three changes shown side by side (a block build taken apart and rebuilt, chocolate melted in a warm-water bath and cooled, an egg cooked by the teacher) | 9 | s2-pieces-new-object; s2-heat-cool-change | 2-PS1-3; 2-PS1-4 | Practices: constructing an evidence-based account; argument from evidence. Crosscutting: energy and matter (the same pieces broken apart and put together into a new, larger object, developed in the account); cause and effect (heating or cooling causes the change); systems | Observations build an account of how an object made of a small set of pieces (blocks, building bricks, a set of shapes, as the clarification lists) can be taken apart and made into a new object: the same pieces, a different whole. Then an argument from evidence that some changes caused by heating or cooling can be reversed and some cannot: water freezes and melts back, butter or chocolate melts and hardens again (reversible); an egg once cooked, a leaf once frozen and thawed, paper once heated stay changed (irreversible), all the clarification's examples. No particle explanation (grade 5) | After unit 3 because the materials and properties just sorted are what the changes act on; the pieces-and-whole account comes first because "can we put it back?" is easiest to see with blocks before heat. Unit 3's water-and-ice record (and unit 1's solid and liquid water) returns as the first heating and cooling evidence (review) | Candidate: settled, drop: melted-crayon bag, zero days; water/chocolate already provide reversible evidence without another heated material | All heating by an adult: a warm-water bath or hot plate kept out of reach, never a flame near students; heated paper shown by the adult or by a no-kit photo set. Check egg, dairy and other food allergies; nothing is eaten. Safety agent's read named in the brief. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 5 | What plants need. *Why is the grass under the board yellow and pale when the grass around it is green?* Anchor: the yellow, pale grass found under a board or tarp left on the lawn for two weeks (or a photograph pair, where the ground is frozen or snow-covered); the dark-shelf plant is kindergarten unit 4's anchor, so it is not used here | 9 | s2-plant-needs-test | 2-LS2-1 | Practices: planning and carrying out a fair investigation; analysing data in a table and a bar graph. Crosscutting: cause and effect | Plan and carry out an investigation of whether plants need sunlight and water to grow: one set of seedlings with light and water, one in the dark, one without water, everything else kept the same, one variable at a time (the boundary). Students measure height to the nearest whole centimeter and draw how the plants look over two to three weeks in a dated table; on one chosen day they make a single-unit bar graph comparing the three conditions (an optional line plot of several plants' heights on that day is a frequency display, not a time graph). The kindergarten claim is tested by experiment | Late winter, so seeds sprout indoors and the test is done by spring. After unit 3, whose fair tests it grows from; it opens with retrieval tasks on kindergarten's needs chart and grade 1's roots and leaves. Before units 6 and 7 because plants that grow are what animals then help and live among | Candidate: settled, drop: cooler fourth treatment, zero days; retain the question without another live comparison | Discard mouldy soil promptly; wash hands after soil. Seeds untreated and not eaten. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 6 | Seeds and pollinators. *How did the burrs get all over my sock, and why do bees visit flower after flower?* Anchor: a sock or fuzzy glove walked through burrs and seed heads, then looked at with a hand lens. Burrs and seed heads are gathered during unit 1 or 2's fall outdoor time and kept dry in a labeled bag for this spring unit; where none grow, a fuzzy glove walked through a tray of hook-and-loop squares stands in, said to be a model | 6 | s2-seed-pollinator-model | 2-LS2-2 | Practice: developing a simple model. Crosscutting: structure and function; systems | A simple model that copies what an animal does to spread seeds or carry pollen: a sock or fuzzy glove walked through burr-like seeds and how far they travel measured in centimeters; a pipe-cleaner "bee" that moves chalk dust from flower to flower. Flower-visit evidence shows bees seeking nectar or pollen for food; pollen can move incidentally between a source and receiving patch. The model represents the animal, relevant plant/seed or flower parts, their relationships and how structure fits the job (system-model). It reverses grade 1's design: there people copied nature; here a model copies the animal's job | Spring, when flowers open and seeds are around. After unit 5 because the plants whose needs were just tested now need animals to spread their seeds; before unit 7 because a habitat's kinds of living things depend on each other this way | Candidate: settled, drop: extra second-animal group model, zero additional days; the independent new-animal model already transfers the learning | No live bees. Check pollen and dust allergies; use large paper pollen pieces, with no chalk, loose dust or drink powders. Burrs kept away from hair and eyes. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 7 | Habitats. *What kinds of plants and animals do we find in each place, and how are the places alike and different?* Anchor: the class's own first survey of two schoolyard spots | 8 | s2-habitat-diversity | 2-LS4-1 | Practices: making observations; analysing data in a graph. Crosscutting: patterns; systems | Observations of plants and animals compare how many different kinds live in different habitats: a schoolyard survey of two spots (lawn and garden bed, or sunny and shady), then photographs and video of a pond, a forest, a desert and an ocean shore. Students keep a picture inventory of each place (sketches, photographs or agreed describing labels such as "small brown beetle"), count the kinds and graph the counts, and compare both how many kinds each place holds and which kinds are shared or different. The emphasis is the diversity in each of several habitats; scientific names are not assessed | Last because it gathers the year: unit 5's plant needs and unit 6's plant/animal relationships raise questions about places, while observations establish which kinds occur; the habitats' land and water recall unit 1's model. After the unit 7 hook, retrieval revisits kindergarten places and resources and grade 1 parts and needs; brief supported repair addresses gaps (no readiness check on day 65). Late spring for the outdoor survey | Candidate: settled, keep: repeated survey on day 70, one existing day, school days 124 and 129 for the two surveys | Outdoor survey rules: touch no unknown plant or animal, stay in sight, return anything moved; check for stinging insects and allergies. Living things are watched, never collected. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |


FILE docs/courses/social-studies-grade-2/frame.md
## Units

| # | Unit and compelling question | Days | Skills | Owned standards | C3 | What students learn | Why this unit, here | Check set adds | Sensitive topics and teacher notes |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Maps tell stories. *Which map shows the world best, and best for what?* ("Can a map ever show the world just as it is?" stays as a supporting question) | 16 | ss2-map-parts; ss2-projections; ss2-make-map; ss2-source-maker | MA.2.T1.1; MA.2.T1.2; MA.2.T1.3; D2.Geo.1.K-2; D2.His.11.K-2; D3.2.K-2 | D2 Geography, D3.2 (who made it, fact and opinion), D4.1 (spoken claim, review) | Title, key, compass rose and scale and what each tells; scale in whole units; Mercator, Peters and the globe compared, and that every map makes choices; a map of a familiar place with all four parts; who made a source, when and where, and fact from opinion, first on maps | Every later unit reads a map, so maps come first; who made a map and why it bends what it bends is the first sourcing question, so source-maker is taught on maps and then used on every source in the year | Candidate: labeling a simple map of North America from memory, with its countries, oceans, Great Lakes, major rivers and mountain ranges (California 2.2.2); maps and globes (Tennessee 2.10). Checked in the unit plan's comparison table | Scale is counted in whole units (blocks, paces), not measured with a ruler (the row's limit; the math lean is grade 1's 1.MD.A.2, equal units laid end to end). No sensitive topic |
| 2 | Land, water and climate. *Where on Earth would you choose to live, and why?* | 13 | ss2-continents-features; ss2-climate-regions; ss2-claim-sources | MA.2.T2.1; MA.2.T2.2; MA.2.T2.3; D2.Geo.4.K-2; D2.Geo.10.K-2; D4.1.K-2 | D2 Geography, D4.1 (claim with a named source) | The seven continents and five oceans placed on a world map and globe, with major features on each continent (the Rocky Mountains and the Great Lakes, the Andes and the Amazon, the Alps and the Danube, the Sahara and the Nile, the Himalayas, the Australian Outback and the Great Barrier Reef, the Antarctic ice sheet) and why oceans matter; how land and water shape weather, climate and what grows; weather and climate; regions; change at two dates in two world regions (D2.Geo.10 in full): physical change in one region (a glacier that shrank, a lake that dried, a forest that grew back) and cultural change in a town or city in another region (one main street in an old and a new photograph: buildings, how people travel, the languages on signs), saying what changed and what stayed, from the sources; a claim with a reason that names its source | Uses unit 1's maps and compass directions; the region model it introduces is what units 3, 4 and 7 use; the written claim with a source is taught here, on the oceans and climates just read, so every later unit closes with it | Candidate: none named from a check state. People and places on each continent seen through present-day photographs is a Curio Class illustration of MA.2.T2.1, not a New York grade 2 requirement (New York's grade 2 compares communities; its world communities are grade 3). Any addition found is recorded with its code in the unit plan's comparison table | Change at two dates: the reason given for a physical change comes from a backed source (values rule b), told at grade level without alarm. No child is asked to choose against where their family lives |
| 3 | Living with the land. *Should people change the land, or change their ways to fit it?* | 16 | ss2-people-environment; ss2-resources-industries; ss2-people-resource | MA.2.T2.4; D2.Geo.5.K-2; D2.Geo.8.K-2; D2.Geo.12.K-2; MA.2.T5.1; MA.2.T5.2; D2.Eco.4.K-2; D2.Eco.7.K-2; D2.Geo.9.K-2; MA.2.T5.3; D2.Eco.13.K-2 | D2 Geography, D2 Economics, D4.6 to D4.8 (the action cycle, at grade 2 depth through the row), D4.1 (review) | How people use, change and adapt to their environment (terraces, dams, cities by rivers, houses on stilts); how city, town and farm communities meet daily needs from near and far; how floods, earthquakes and hurricanes affect people and how they help each other recover; natural resources and the industries and jobs they support, here and in the countries studied; renewable and non-renewable resources; goods and services made here and elsewhere and what it costs to make a good; why people settle by a river or a landform; people as a resource, tools and machines; the year's action cycle (storm readiness for the school) | Uses unit 2's regions (life fits the place); resources and industries belong with the land that gives them, so the economics rows sit here; the action cycle falls in the first half of the year so the class has time to carry it out and look back | Candidate: following one food from farm to table, with its producers and consumers (California 2.4.1); comparing urban, suburban and rural communities (New York 2.1); resource-industry examples informed by Tennessee 2.04 (its wider industry survey not adopted). Checked in the unit plan's comparison table | A child may have lived through a disaster: examples are told calmly and end with how people helped; no child is asked to tell their own story. The readiness product is checked against the town's or state's published advice, never invented safety rules. Action is the class's choice, carried out through the school |
| 4 | Countries and trade. *Why do countries need each other?* | 10 | ss2-countries; ss2-trade-world | MA.2.T4.1; MA.2.T4.2; D2.Eco.14.K-2; D2.Eco.15.K-2; D2.Geo.11.K-2 | D2 Civics, D2 Geography, D2 Economics, D4.1 (review) | Physical and political geography (Africa as one continent and more than 50 countries); what makes a country (land and borders, people, a government, laws, usually a capital and a flag); the purposes of government (keeping people safe, providing schools and roads); two countries compared by their history, culture, language and way of governing; every country has a government, but not every government is chosen in elections (MA.2.T4.2 with its clarification); physical and political maps of one continent side by side; why people in one country trade with another; things at home made abroad and things made here and sold abroad, traced on a world map; opportunity cost again in trade | After unit 3, because trade follows from the resources, skills and products places have; it uses grade 1's idea of groups and government, then distinguishes that idea from the different ways countries choose leaders and govern; the country idea is what unit 7's report needs | Candidate: how countries work together to solve problems (trade agreements, treaties) at grade level (California 2.3.2); maps and trade (Tennessee 2.11, 2.07 to 2.08). Checked in the unit plan's comparison table | Borders and countries told from current political maps; disputed borders are not taught at this age. Products traced are everyday goods, never brands named in student copy |
| 5 | People on the move. *Why do people leave home for a new place?* | 14 | ss2-why-people-move; ss2-nation-of-immigrants; ss2-two-accounts | MA.2.T3.1; D2.Geo.7.K-2; D2.His.14.K-2; D2.His.16.K-2; MA.2.T3.2; D2.His.4.K-2; D2.His.6.K-2 | D2 History, D2 Geography, D3 (sourcing, review), D4.1 (claim with a named source) | Why people move (work, family, safety, land, freedom); chosen and forced moves; the cause-effect chain; why the United States is called "a nation of immigrants" and its honest limits (Native nations were here first and are not immigrants; enslaved Africans did not choose to come); what newcomers long ago (Ellis Island, Angel Island) and today say about arriving; two accounts of one arrival compared, with the check that each teller had their own way of knowing; people, goods and ideas all move (D2.Geo.7): goods were traced in unit 4, and what moves with people (foods, words, music) is unit 6 | Comes after the world year's geography so moves can be traced on maps between continents and countries; it introduces the cause-effect chain unit 6 needs and uses unit 1's sourcing on two accounts | Candidate: none named from a check state. Comparing newcomers at Ellis Island and Angel Island side by side is a Curio Class example, each source chosen and credited at the item level; any check-set addition is recorded with its code in the unit plan's comparison table | Forced moves named plainly at grade level: Africans captured, brought in chains and enslaved; the Cherokee forced from their homelands by the United States government in the 1830s, with the Cherokee Nation and the United Keetoowah Band governing themselves today in Oklahoma and the Eastern Band in its North Carolina homeland; refugees fleeing war today. Angel Island: some newcomers, most of them Chinese, were held for weeks, months, and for some people up to two years, because a law kept most Chinese people out, told plainly. No role play or simulation of capture, removal or flight. Some children are immigrants or refugees: no child is asked to tell a family story. Hard history and Native content: flagged for the sensitivity read |
| 6 | Family stories and culture. *When people move to a new place, do they change the place more, or does the place change them more?* | 10 | ss2-family-interviews; ss2-culture-migration | MA.2.T3.3; D2.His.12.K-2; MA.2.T3.4 | D2 History, D1.5 (questions for an interview, review), D4.1 (review) | Supporting questions: what do people bring with them when they move; how do newcomers change; how does the place they move to change. Writing interview questions; interviewing a family member, neighbor or school staff member about where their family came from, how, why and when they came to this town or state; an interview as a source, what it can and cannot tell; answers on a timeline; what makes up a culture, what people bring when they move, how migration changes newcomers and the place, and how all of a community's people (newcomers, long-time residents, Native nations) make it richer; the unit closes with a modeled claim from the supplied sources and interviews, never a ranking of cultures | Follows unit 5 because the interviews ask the questions unit 5 taught (why, how, when people moved) and culture change is the "what happened next" of the cause-effect chain | Candidate: building a family or class history time line from primary and secondary sources (California 2.1.1); customs compared and a shared community goal, informed by Tennessee 2.01 and 2.02 (inside days 77 and 78). Checked in the unit plan's comparison table | Families differ (adoption, foster care, a refugee story, an unknown past): a student may interview any trusted adult, including school staff, and no one is asked about immigration status. No family's culture is presented as the standard one, and no student is asked to disclose a private family experience to answer the question |
| 7 | A country up close. *What matters most about how people live in the country I chose?* | 12 | ss2-country-report | MA.2.T4.3; D1.4.K-2; D3.1.K-2; D4.3.K-2 | D1.4 (supporting questions), D3.1 (gathering facts from chosen sources), D4.3 (presenting an argument), D4.1 (review) | Each student chooses a country from a menu of about 16 countries outside the United States (at least two from each inhabited continent; Ghana, Canada and Mexico left off), each with a Curio Class source set (one map, two short captioned pages at a stated grade 2 difficulty, present-day photographs, rights lines); writes supporting questions tied to the big one, gathers facts from one or two sources picked for what they are and how they are built, and presents a short report on its land, resources and people today, with a map, ending in one claim backed by two facts from the sources, presented in print, aloud and in a digital version made with an adult on the class's one shared device (the printed report is the paper path) | The year's gathering: it uses maps (unit 1), continents and regions (unit 2), resources (unit 3), what makes a country (unit 4) and culture (unit 6), and the claim with a named source (unit 2); last so every part is in hand | Candidate: none beyond MA.2.T4.3, which the report already meets. Comparing one custom with the student's own community is a Curio Class choice; any check-set addition found is recorded with its code in the unit plan's comparison table | People shown in the present day, in cities and towns as well as the countryside; no stereotypes. The menu leaves off Ghana (grade 3's unit 3 compares a Ghanaian market town) and Canada and Mexico (grade 4 researches them, ss4-canada-mexico-research). A family country not on the menu is welcome with the teacher's help, never required, and no student must share why they chose it. Every menu country has its own source set, so no student needs a library or the open web |


